sub:assertion {
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Saturated Schnakenberg Turing wavelength for Dugesia japonica dorsal pigment at 6.912 µm (Holocene, Pass 1) faithfully reproduces Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) morphology" ;
        dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
        dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
        pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/dugesia_japonica_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian); environment: python 3.12, lean not installed in this VM; theorems are Lean 4 prelude, unchecked here; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing (1952) gives the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber from the reaction Jacobian and the two diffusivities. | Schnakenberg (1979) gives the polynomial activator-substrate kinetics; Gierer & Meinhardt (1972) and Murray (1981) are the pigment-pattern reading. | Nogi, Zhang, Chan & Marchant (2009) published the CC BY 2.5 plate of regenerated Dugesia japonica with dorsal pigment and ocelli. | Orii, Ito & Watanabe (2002) describe extant Dugesia japonica anatomy. Herath & Lobo (2020) model planarian fission with Turing patterns, not this wavelength.; reconstructed_in_script: κ = 0.15 saturation, R = D_v/D_u = 48, and D_u solved so λ_c = 6.912 µm on a 48.384 µm patch (seven linear wavelengths). | Spectral diffusion plus explicit reaction, seed 3213, and the linear-mode Pearson comparison. | Body mask and ocellus cups segmented from the Nogi plate and used as the display domain.; transfer_boundary: Fish pigment waves (Kondo 2009) and Schmidtea eye regeneration (Lapan & Reddien 2012) are not measurements of a 6.912 µm Dugesia japonica mode. | The Nogi plate is a praziquantel-regeneration micrograph without a scale bar. Pixel size is not 6.912 µm.; does_not_claim: Does not claim that 6.912 µm was measured on Dugesia japonica in any of the cited papers. It is this slot's detail-scale operating point. | Does not claim a fossil predecessor. The chronoseries is Holocene 1/1, age 0 Ma. | Does not claim the rendered body outlines are a pixel-perfect copy of four separate worms; the brightness mask merges some animals.]; phenomenon_size: 8.9 µm (8.928e-06 m, log10=-5.049 m, tier=3_subcellular_um); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 8.9 µm to 2.6 cm): 8.9 µm:dugesia-japonica-bioelectric-polarity, 32 µm:dugesia-japonica-vertex-packing, 581 µm:dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength, 1.1 mm:dugesia-japonica-clock-wavefront, 1.9 mm:dugesia-japonica-morphogen-condensation, 2.4 mm:dugesia-japonica-flux-canalization, 2.6 mm:dugesia-japonica-elastic-buckling, 3.0 mm:dugesia-japonica-branching-tree" ;
        pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00289234>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(79)90042-0>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(81)90334-9>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2012.06.018>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jtbi.2019.110042>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1952.0012>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pntd.0000464>,
            <https://doi.org/10.1387/ijdb.072502sk>,
            <https://doi.org/10.2108/zsj.19.1123> .
    <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py> a schema:SoftwareSourceCode ;
        rdfs:label "models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py" ;
        schema:codeRepository <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib> ;
        schema:programmingLanguage "Python" .
}

